# Writing-style cards — Ba–Y–Zn–Si–O silicate phase-diagram reviews

Corpus: 32 screened review exemplars (15 `core`, 10 `adjacent`, 7 `method`); 10 PDFs are archived in `pdfs/` and were read with `pdftotext`. This is a cross-domain benchmark because no large set of Ba–Y–Zn–Si–O review articles exists. Cards describe structure and sentence form only; they do not authorize citations in the manuscript.

## 1. Structure skeleton cards

**S1 — motivation → organizing axis → branch synthesis → bottlenecks → outlook (9/10 full texts).** The modal sequence is an application or materials need in the opening, a definition of the organizing variable (phase, composition, growth route, or model), branch-by-branch evidence sections, a dedicated limitations/challenges section, and a short forward-looking close. Sources: W2024048721, W2116988729, W2806710761, W3110875303, W3031330708, W2891727177, W3206007668, W2607375921, W3152601855.

**S2 — fundamentals before taxonomy (8/10).** Thermodynamic definitions/model assumptions precede system comparisons in CALPHAD reviews, while crystal-growth reviews define the growth mechanism before listing materials. Sources: W2888057454, W2116988729, W2806710761, W3110875303, W3031330708, W2024048721, W2607375921, W3152601855.

**S3 — observed section skeletons (verbatim headings normalized only for punctuation).**

| source ID | section order seen in PDF |
|---|---|
| W2024048721 | Introduction → Crystalline inorganic electrolytes (LISICON, garnet, perovskite, NASICON) → Glass-based inorganic electrolytes → conductivity/interface strategies → conclusion |
| W2888057454 | Introduction → substitutional regular solution → compound-energy formalism → short/long-range ordering and TCP phases → applications/examples → Summary |
